// Progression gates (docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md §5) — the content that decides
// WHEN a tier of the game becomes available, and what it makes available.
// Data, never hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron`,
// `machines.ron`, `shop.ron`, `ingredients.ron` and `contracts.ron`; the
// loader is `sim_core::sim::unlocks::UnlockRegistry`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way every other data file's loader does.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until it did, the game gated NOTHING: a new game
// could buy every ingredient and sign every contract in `contracts.ron`,
// so the only thing that grew as you played was the balance. That was fine
// while there was one sauce — there was nothing to hold back. BBQ is the
// second sauce, and handing a player the whole two-sauce board on their
// first minute makes the ketchup line look like a chore standing between
// them and the interesting one. So BBQ arrives, with an announcement, once
// the ketchup line has demonstrably run — and "demonstrably" means it has
// SOLD KETCHUP, by the box. Mustard arrives the same way off BBQ: each sauce
// is earned by the sauce before it, so the tiers are a chain and not a
// stopwatch.
//
// WHAT A GATE GATES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. An `Unlock` names
// two lists and only two: `ingredients`, the ids removed from the market
// (`data/ingredients.ron`) until it opens, and `contracts`, the ids removed
// from the contracts board (`data/contracts.ron`) until it opens. Both are
// enforced in the simulation — `BuyIngredient` and `SignContract` are
// REFUSED for a locked id, not merely hidden from a panel — so a gate is a
// rule rather than a decoration on the UI.
//
// **NO RECIPE IS EVER LOCKED, and there is no field here to lock one with.**
// The BBQ recipes sit in `data/recipes.ron` from a new game. They are simply
// unbuildable (their raws cannot be bought) and unsellable (nobody is
// asking). That is the whole reason this shape was chosen over an
// `unlocked: bool` on a recipe: a locked recipe would be a state every
// placed machine consults on every tick, on the hottest path in the
// simulation, to answer a question that changes at most a handful of times
// in a session. A gate that is a filter on two lists costs the tick nothing
// at all.
//
// WHY THE GROUP IS NAMED, RATHER THAN EACH ENTRY CARRYING ITS OWN
// `unlock_after`. The announcement is the point of the gate — a gate that
// opens silently is a gate the player never experiences — and an
// announcement needs a subject. "BBQ Sauce unlocked" is a sentence;
// "spice_mix unlocked, molasses unlocked, liquid_smoke unlocked, smokehouse
// trial unlocked, …" is a log. `name` is what the toast says, so it is
// player-facing prose and not an id.
//
// THE TRIGGER IS BOXES OF A NAMED PRODUCT SOLD (the owner's rule,
// 2026-09-26). Each gate names an item and a count: BBQ opens after 100
// boxes of `boxed_ketchup` have been sold, mustard after 100 boxes of
// `boxed_bbq`. "Sold" is a box handed over as part of a satisfied delivery
// window — the dock being PAID is what selling means in this game
// (`Economy::tick`'s `Paid` event, which now adds each window's mix to
// `lifetime_boxes_sold` box by box). A missed window sells nothing, and a
// lost contract does not take its past sales back. The ledger is lifetime and
// never decreases, so a gate, once open, stays open.
//
// THIS REVERSES WHAT THIS HEADER USED TO ARGUE, and the old argument was
// wrong on its own terms. It said a delivery "is proof that a whole line ran
// end to end and something was shipped" — true, but a delivery window counted
// exactly the same whether it carried one box or eight, so the number it fed
// the gates measured how many windows had elapsed and not how much the
// factory made. Worse, it was blind to WHICH line ran: a player shipping
// nothing but ketchup and a player shipping nothing but BBQ reached the
// mustard gate at the same moment. Boxes of a named product fix both. They
// scale with output, and they make each tier's gate depend on the line before
// it actually running, which is the progression the tiers were drawn as.
//
// FIELDS, per unlock:
//   id           stable content id (the save format does not key on it — see
//                below — but tests and the toast's diff do)
//   name         what the player reads in the announcement
//   after_sold   (item: "<id>", boxes: N) — the product whose sales open this
//                gate, and how many boxes of it. ONE field holding both,
//                because a count without a product, or a product without a
//                count, is not a gate. `item` must be an id something in
//                `data/recipes.ron` PRODUCES *and* that some contract in
//                `data/contracts.ron` ORDERS — only a paid window's `mix`
//                feeds the boxes-sold ledger, so a gate keyed to anything
//                else could never open — and `boxes` must be at least 1,
//                since 0 would be a gate that is open in a new game and
//                therefore not a gate. WHERE EACH IS CAUGHT: `boxes: 0` is
//                refused by the loader itself (`from_ron_str`), so it is a
//                startup panic. The `item` rules are `validate_covers`,
//                which `sim_core`'s test suite runs against these shipped
//                files — a cross-file check is a test here, not a boot-time
//                panic (docs/DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-08 and 2026-09-26). Edit
//                `item` wrongly and `cargo test` fails; the game itself
//                would boot and simply never open the tier.
//   ingredients  `data/ingredients.ron` ids held back until it opens
//   contracts    `data/contracts.ron` ids held back until it opens
//
// At least one of the two lists must be non-empty: an unlock that releases
// nothing is content that does nothing, and the loader rejects it rather
// than shipping a gate with no effect. No id may be gated by two unlocks.
//
// FILE ORDER IS TIER ORDER, and since the trigger changed it is the ONLY
// thing that says so. Two thresholds counting different products are not two
// numbers you can compare — 100 boxes of ketchup is neither more nor less
// than 100 boxes of BBQ — so "the first gate" and "the deepest gate", which
// the Cookbook both needs, are read off the order of the list below. Write a
// new tier after the tier that feeds it.
//
// WHAT IS SAVED. The ledger, and nothing else — `SaveData` stores
// `lifetime_boxes_sold` and derives every gate's state from it against THIS
// file. Saving "bbq_sauce is unlocked" instead would freeze a save against
// the numbers in this file on the day it was written; deriving it means a
// balance pass here re-reads correctly for every existing save.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    unlocks: [
        // THE FIRST GATE IN THE GAME (docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md §5, the BBQ
        // epic, 2026-09-18; re-triggered on boxes sold 2026-09-26).
        //
        // ONE HUNDRED BOXES OF KETCHUP, because that is the owner's rule:
        // the second sauce is the reward for a ketchup line that has actually
        // SOLD ketchup. A hundred boxes is a line that was built, fed,
        // staffed and then left running — not one that survived a fixed
        // number of windows, which is what the old trigger measured.
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS IN TIME, measured 2026-09-26 (headless:
        // `reference_chain_with_budget` plus the stocked larder of
        // `sim_core/tests/common`, raws topped back up as a careful player
        // does, at ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 30 ticks/sec):
        //
        //   1 order (corner_shop signed  100th box at tick 52 000 — 29 min,
        //   by hand — a new game since    50 delivery windows
        //   2026-09-26 ships NO contract
        //   signed at all, so this is the
        //   cheapest a real run can be)
        //   3 orders signed at tick 0     100th box at tick 33 000 — 18 min,
        //                                 44 windows, 1 630 in the bank
        //
        // SO THIS GATE IS MUCH LATER THAN IT WAS, and the owner asked for the
        // count knowing the count, not the clock: ten windows was about three
        // minutes. Eighteen is still one session, but it is no longer "the
        // first few minutes", and it is later than the MUSTARD gate used to
        // arrive (25 windows, about 11 minutes — see that entry). If either
        // tier now wants to land sooner, the fix is this file: a smaller
        // `boxes`, or a per-window demand raise in `data/contracts.ron`.
        //
        // WHAT THAT DOES TO ISSUE #55, which the sweep below is the record
        // of. #55 is that building the BBQ branch starves the ketchup line
        // while two new pipe runs and a vat prime, which is several missed
        // windows against three-strike contracts: the reward for building the
        // thing the game just unlocked is losing the order that paid for it.
        // The sweep's own table says the trap stops biting at about forty
        // windows, and BOTH rows above are past it: forty-four windows with
        // three orders signed at tick 0, fifty with corner_shop alone — and
        // fifty is the row a real run walks, since a new game signs nothing.
        // So a gate chosen for a different reason has largely closed #55's
        // window as a side effect. #55 STAYS OPEN anyway: those rows are where
        // a fed, staffed line arrives, not where every player does, a player
        // who reaches the gate slowly still arrives with little banked, and the
        // real fixes are still its own (#92, #93). Nothing here was tuned for
        // it.
        //
        // ==== THE 2026-09-20 SWEEP, IN THE OLD UNIT ====
        // Everything below was measured when the trigger was LIFETIME
        // DELIVERY WINDOWS, and its numbers are windows, not boxes. It is
        // kept because its finding is about the factory and not about the
        // trigger: what decides whether the branch costs you the board is the
        // BANK at the moment of building.
        //
        // THE PROTOCOL, because every number below moves if it changes.
        // Each run: build the shipped ketchup line through real purchase and
        // place commands; fund it; stock the larder with 1 200 of each raw;
        // leave hopper and tank auto-fill on; hire until fully staffed; sign
        // the starter contracts AT THE STATED MOMENT; wait for the stated
        // lifetime-delivery count; build the BBQ branch and its mixed exit;
        // then settle 15 000 ticks and read the board. "Bank" is finished
        // boxed_ketchup on hand at the moment the branch goes in. A gate
        // value other than ten is EMULATED by waiting for that many lifetime
        // deliveries before building — the shipped gate is long open by then
        // and nothing else reads the number, so it is exactly equivalent.
        //
        //   what the transient costs  boxed_ketchup output stops for about
        //                             8 500 ticks; roughly 35 boxes' worth of
        //                             throughput is absorbed into the new
        //                             pipes and vat before any comes back out
        //   1 order, gate 10          bank 22 — nothing lost
        //   3 orders signed AT THE
        //     GATE, gate 10           bank 22 — diner_supply lost
        //   3 orders signed at tick
        //     0, gate 10              bank 7 — corner_shop and diner_supply
        //                             lost to the branch
        //   emulated gate 20          bank 12 — 2 lost
        //   emulated gate 30          bank 19 — 1 lost
        //   emulated gate 40          bank 24 — NOTHING lost to the branch
        //
        // THE CONTROL MATTERS: with three orders signed at tick 0 and NO
        // branch ever built, market_stall still strikes out at tick 1 500.
        // That death is the ketchup line starting up, not the BBQ branch, and
        // it is why the "3 orders at tick 0" rows above count only what the
        // branch took.
        //
        // WHAT THE SWEEP ACTUALLY SHOWS. The deciding variable is the BANK AT
        // THE MOMENT OF BUILDING — not the number of concurrent orders, which
        // matters only through the bank it leaves. A delivery count steers
        // that bank by proxy and badly, because signing more contracts is both
        // how a player reaches the count sooner and what makes the transient
        // cost more. Pushed far enough the gate DOES work: forty deliveries
        // leaves the three-order player 24 banked and costs them nothing. But
        // forty is not "inside a first session", which is the entire reason
        // ten was chosen above, and a gate bought that way would be paying for
        // a transient with the thing the gate exists to deliver.
        //
        // That sweep concluded TEN WINDOWS, kept because raising it to a
        // value that would fix #55 cost more than #55 did — forty windows was
        // not "inside a first session", which was ten's whole justification.
        // The 2026-09-26 trigger change reaches forty-four by another route,
        // and it is worth being plain that this is luck rather than design:
        // nobody sized 100 boxes against #55. The fixes #55 wants are still
        // its own first option — say so at placement time, before it costs
        // anything, which the simulation has the information to do and no
        // surface to say it on (issue #92) — or a fluid network that will not
        // let a brand-new consumer take 100% of a network the old one is
        // living on (issue #93). Both are outside this file. Full records:
        // `docs/DECISIONS.md`, 2026-09-20, "The bank at the moment of building
        // decides whether the BBQ branch costs you the board", and 2026-09-26,
        // "A tier is earned by selling the tier below it".
        Unlock(
            id: "bbq_sauce",
            name: "BBQ Sauce",
            after_sold: (item: "boxed_ketchup", boxes: 100),
            // The BBQ branch's three raws (`data/ingredients.ron`).
            // Finished ketchup is its fourth input and is NOT here: the
            // factory makes it, so it was never on the market to hold back.
            ingredients: ["spice_mix", "molasses", "liquid_smoke"],
            // Every contract that asks for BBQ, including `grill_pub` and
            // `roadhouse_group`, which ask for ketchup as well. A mixed
            // order is still a BBQ order — signing one before the branch
            // exists would be signing up to strike out.
            contracts: ["smokehouse_trial", "grill_pub", "roadhouse_group", "bbq_festival"],
        ),

        // THE SECOND GATE (the mustard epic, 2026-09-26; re-triggered on
        // boxes sold the same day), and it is holding back a different KIND of
        // thing from the first one.
        //
        // The BBQ gate holds back a branch: four recipes that hang off a line
        // the player has already built and paid for, so what it waits for is
        // understanding. This one holds back a whole second line from raw —
        // five machines, two hoppers, a tank, and the belt and pipe to join
        // them — so what it has to wait for is MONEY. A gate that opens onto
        // a tier the bank cannot afford is worse than no gate: it is an
        // announcement followed by nothing the player can act on.
        //
        // ONE HUNDRED BOXES OF BBQ, because that is the owner's rule and
        // because it is the same sentence one tier down: mustard is earned by
        // selling BBQ, exactly as BBQ is earned by selling ketchup. It also
        // settles the affordability question above by construction rather than
        // by a number — a player who has SOLD a hundred cases of BBQ has been
        // paid for them, and a mustard line costs 959.
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS IN TIME, measured from a NEW GAME on 2026-09-29 by
        // `godot_project/measure_gate_ticks.gd`, re-measured 2026-10-02 when a
        // case became eight bottles (fully staffed, raws topped
        // up and money no object — a floor, not a typical run):
        //
        //   100th box of boxed_ketchup at tick 52 000, and the 100th box of
        //   boxed_bbq at tick 131 000 — 73 minutes at 30 ticks/sec, of which
        //   79 000 ticks (~44 min) are after the first gate opened, with
        //   every one of the four BBQ orders signed
        //
        //   (the tool prints "64 000 ticks" for that gap because it starts
        //   its clock at the fixture's hand-off at tick 67 000, 15 000 ticks
        //   of branch-building and settling after the gate itself opened)
        //
        // THAT IS A FLAG, not a measurement in support: it puts mustard about
        // an hour and a quarter into a session, where the old trigger put it
        // at eleven minutes. The cause is not the factory — with all four BBQ
        // orders signed the board asks for about 4.5 boxes per 1 000 ticks
        // (smokehouse_trial 2 boxes / 2 500 ticks = 0.80, grill_pub 2/3 000 =
        // 0.67, roadhouse_group 4/4 000 = 1.00, bbq_festival 10/5 000 = 2.00)
        // and the branch put about 1.4 of them into paid windows (92 boxes in
        // the 64 000 ticks measured; it was 1.0 and 88 000 ticks while a case
        // was one bottle, when the 60-tick ketchup bottler took the vat's
        // output before the BBQ mixer could), so it is the branch's throughput against the cadences
        // the orders keep, not a shortage of demand. Whoever wants
        // mustard sooner has three content-side levers and no Rust to write:
        // a smaller `boxes` here, more BBQ demand in `data/contracts.ron`, or
        // a faster BBQ recipe.
        //
        // ==== THE 2026-09-26 AFFORDABILITY SWEEP, IN THE OLD UNIT ====
        // Measured when the trigger was LIFETIME DELIVERY WINDOWS; its
        // numbers are windows. Kept because what it establishes — what a
        // mustard line costs, and that the one-order player's balance FALLS
        // rather than grows — is about the economy and not about the trigger.
        // Same shape of protocol as the BBQ gate's, one substitution: "bank"
        // here is the MONEY BALANCE, not boxed_ketchup on hand, because a
        // mustard line is bought rather than plumbed off an existing one.
        //
        // THE PROTOCOL. Build the shipped ketchup line through real purchase
        // and place commands (`sim::reference_chain_with_budget`, which prices
        // its own bill out of `data/shop.ron`); leave the remainder of
        // `sim::STARTING_MONEY` in the bank; hopper and tank auto-fill ON;
        // sign the stated orders at tick 0; then buy raws like a careful
        // player — top each one back up to the 20 units `data/storage.ron`
        // gives for free, and never spend below 30, because wages come first.
        // Read the balance at each lifetime-delivery mark. A gate value other
        // than 25 is EMULATED by reading the balance at that many deliveries:
        // nothing but this file reads the number, so it is exactly
        // equivalent.
        //
        // WHAT A MUSTARD LINE COSTS, out of `data/shop.ron`: 20 belt at 5,
        // 8 pipe at 8, 2 hopper at 40, 1 fluid_tank at 60, crusher 75,
        // mixer 100, cooking_vat 200, bottler 120, packer 90, dock 70 =
        // **959** — the same kit as the ketchup line, because it is the same
        // five stages in the same order. (A player who already owns a dock
        // and can reach it pays 889; the gate is sized against the whole
        // thing.) For contrast, the BBQ branch is 872 on top of a line that
        // already exists, and `STARTING_MONEY` leaves 2 041 after the first
        // line's 959.
        //
        //   1 order (corner_shop signed  10 deliveries  734
        //   at tick 0, 40)               25 deliveries  597
        //                                40 deliveries  449
        //   3 starter orders signed at   10 deliveries  828
        //   tick 0 (250 in signatures)   15 deliveries  962
        //                                20 deliveries  1 039
        //                                25 deliveries  1 176  <- the old gate
        //                                30 deliveries  1 231
        //                                40 deliveries  1 435
        //
        // These rows were measured when a new game shipped with corner_shop
        // already signed for free; since 2026-09-26 (DECISIONS.md) nothing is
        // signed for the player, so every row here is the measured balance
        // less corner_shop's 40 signature. Nothing else in the protocol moves:
        // no row comes near the 30 floor the raw-buying rule keeps, so the
        // 40 is a flat offset rather than a change in what the player buys.
        //
        // The table counts DELIVERIES because that is what the gate counted
        // when it was measured. The trigger is boxes sold now, so no row here
        // is a gate any more — what the table still shows is the shape of the
        // balance as the factory runs, which is the question it was built to
        // answer.
        //
        // WHAT THAT SHOWS, and it is two different things.
        //
        // (1) For the player the board is built for — all three starter
        // orders signed, which `contracts.ron`'s balance note says is the
        // intended first several minutes — 25 deliveries leaves 1 176 against
        // a 959 line, arriving at about tick 20 000 (eleven minutes at
        // ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 30 ticks/sec). The line is affordable with
        // room for the mistakes, and it is affordable from about the 15th
        // delivery onwards. That was where the old gate sat, and it is the
        // number the boxes-sold trigger replaced; the affordability finding
        // survives it, because the balance only ever grows from there for
        // this player, and the boxes-sold gate lands much later still.
        //
        // (2) For the one-order player the balance FALLS, by roughly 10 per
        // delivery, and no gate value on this table ever reaches 959. That is
        // not the gate's problem and moving the gate cannot fix it: a factory
        // serving a single 2-box order overproduces into a delivery pool
        // nobody is buying from, which is exactly the loss
        // `contracts.ron`'s header describes, and the fix is signing the
        // other two starter orders (150 and 60, a window or two of payouts).
        // Raising the gate would make that player wait longer for a tier they
        // still could not afford, while making the intended player wait for
        // nothing.
        //
        // Growth past the gate is only about +13 per delivery (+27 per 1 000
        // ticks), so no nearby value materially changes affordability either
        // way — 20 would already do, 30 would cost a minute. The same control
        // the BBQ protocol records still holds: with three orders signed at
        // tick 0 and no second line ever built, `market_stall` strikes out
        // during start-up, so its death belongs to the ketchup line warming
        // up rather than to anything mustard does.
        //
        // WHAT IS NOT MEASURED HERE, honestly. These runs do not build the
        // mustard line and settle it, and they do not stack a BBQ branch's
        // 872 on top; a player who has just bought a branch is transiently
        // short, and the fix for that is the same one issue #55 wants
        // (#92/#93), not a different number in this file. The mustard line
        // has one advantage the branch does not, which is why it is not
        // another #55: it shares nothing with the ketchup line except
        // `vinegar`, so building it cannot starve a running line of anything
        // but bought stock.
        Unlock(
            id: "mustard_sauce",
            name: "Mustard",
            after_sold: (item: "boxed_bbq", boxes: 100),
            // Mustard's two raws (`data/ingredients.ron`). `vinegar` is NOT
            // here and must not be: the ketchup mixer drinks it from tick 0,
            // so gating it would lock the shipped line out of its own
            // ingredient. That is the one thing this gate cannot hold back,
            // and it is also the thing that makes the tier interesting.
            ingredients: ["mustard_seed", "turmeric"],
            // Every contract that asks for mustard, including `deli_chain`,
            // which asks for ketchup as well — a mixed order is still a
            // mustard order, and signing one without a mustard line is
            // signing up to strike out.
            contracts: ["deli_counter", "hot_dog_stand", "deli_chain", "sandwich_franchise"],
        ),
    ],
)
